WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.935
This section asks one question.

00:00:04.935 --> 00:00:07.870
When is concurrent execution correct?

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:10.805
The test is a graph.

00:00:10.805 --> 00:00:13.740
You draw a directed graph from a schedule, and you check it for a cycle.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.675
So before the transactions, two graph ideas, quickly.

00:00:16.675 --> 00:00:20.174
A directed graph has no cycle when you follow the arrows and never return to a node you have already visited.

00:00:20.174 --> 00:00:23.672
That is the DAG on the left.

00:00:23.672 --> 00:00:27.171
It is cyclic when some path loops back on itself, the graph on the right.

00:00:27.171 --> 00:00:30.669
That one distinction is the whole test ahead.

00:00:30.669 --> 00:00:34.168
No cycle means safe.

00:00:34.168 --> 00:00:37.666
A cycle means trouble.

00:00:37.666 --> 00:00:40.907
Here is a DAG, a directed acyclic graph.

00:00:40.907 --> 00:00:44.149
A topological sort puts its nodes in an order where, for every edge A to B, A comes before B.

00:00:44.149 --> 00:00:47.390
Think of getting dressed.

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:50.632
Socks before shoes.

00:00:50.632 --> 00:00:53.873
Pants before belt.

00:00:53.873 --> 00:00:57.115
If the rules also demanded shoes before socks, no order would work.

00:00:57.115 --> 00:01:00.356
That impossible case is a cycle.

00:01:00.356 --> 00:01:03.598
A graph with no cycle always has at least one valid order.

00:01:03.598 --> 00:01:06.840
This one has two, because B and C are independent.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:10.081
A graph with a cycle has none.

00:01:10.081 --> 00:01:13.669
That is the whole toolkit.

00:01:13.669 --> 00:01:17.256
No cycle, lay it in a line.

00:01:17.256 --> 00:01:20.844
A cycle, no line exists.

00:01:20.844 --> 00:01:24.431
Next, we build one of these graphs from a transaction schedule and read its cycles to decide when concurrent execution stays correct.
